<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Dušan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1308–<a href="/years/1355">1355</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Stefan Uroš IV Dušan, Dušan the Mighty · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Dušan was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1331 and was crowned Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in 1346. He conquered much of Byzantine Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, raised the Serbian church to a patriarchate and issued the law code known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusans-code">Dušan's Code</a> in 1349. His empire was the largest Balkan state of its day but broke apart among regional lords soon after his death in 1355.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan.jpg" alt="Stefan Dušan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown 14th century Serbian artist · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Uro%C5%A1_IV_Du%C5%A1an_of_Serbia_Lesnovo_fresco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Dušan was the eldest son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-decanski">Stefan Dečanski</a> and Theodora Smilets, daughter of the Bulgarian emperor Smilets. In 1314 his father quarrelled with his own father, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a>, and was exiled; the family lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> until his recall in 1320. There Dušan became acquainted with Byzantine culture and learned Greek. After a civil war Dečanski was crowned on 6 January 1322, and Dušan was crowned young king and later given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, which marked him as the intended heir.</p><p>In his youth Dušan fought in two battles, defeating Bosnian forces in 1329 during the War of Hum and the Bulgarian emperor Michael III Shishman at Velbužd in 1330. He became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 8 September 1331 after the struggle with his father, and sealed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> by marrying Helen, sister of Ivan Alexander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 16 April 1346 Dušan convoked an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, attended by the Serbian archbishop, the Archbishop of Ochrid, the Bulgarian patriarch and clerics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. It raised the Serbian archbishopric to a patriarchate, and the first patriarch, Joanikije II, crowned Dušan Emperor and autocrat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, a title soon enlarged to include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>. He had his son Uroš crowned king of the Serbs and Greeks. Dušan gave Byzantine court titles to his nobles, such as despotes, sebastocrator and caesar, and was the first Serbian monarch to write most of his letters in Greek.</p><p>In 1347 he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>, placing his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-uros">Simeon Uroš</a> over them, and in 1348 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, under the governor Preljub. He then sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, for which he needed a fleet, and negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which politely declined a military alliance. In 1349 he introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusans-code">Dušan's Code</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1354 the Hungarians occupied part of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Dušan repelled the invasion, and peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> followed in May 1355. He had intended to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and lead a crusading army against the Turks, but died suddenly on 20 December 1355 and was buried in his foundation near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>. His remains were later moved to the Church of Saint Mark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. His empire, spanning a large territory, slowly crumbled under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> as regional aristocrats drew away from central rule.</p><p>Stefan Dušan is remembered as the most powerful Serbian ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and remains a folk hero to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. The aim of restoring his empire stayed an ideal for Serbs under Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Dušan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Stefan Dušan</b> did not fight a single field battle to win his empire: he took it by besieging cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stefan Dušan</b>, King of Serbia since 1331, was crowned Emperor of the Serbs and Greeks at Skopje, founding the Serbian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Serbian civil war, <b>Stefan Dušan</b> took Petrič after a siege and imprisoned his father, King Stefan Uroš III Dečanski, in Zvečan Fortress, where he died in November.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Serbia under <b>Stefan Dushan</b> annexed Epirus in 1348.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Stefan Dušan's</b> death in 1355, his widow, Empress Jelena, ruled Serres, the city he had taken from Byzantium in 1345.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-serres">Despotate of Serres</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stefan Dušan</b>, who took the throne in 1331, conquered much of Byzantine Macedonia, Albania and Epirus and in 1346 was crowned emperor at Skopje, turning the kingdom into the Serbian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Devoll District, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.58,20.93&amp;year=1338" aria-label="Devoll District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5063 714 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Stefan Dušan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5513 984h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5513 984h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.58,20.93&amp;year=1338">What was here in 1338? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
